http {
    log_format  main  '$remote_addr - $remote_user [$time_local] "$request" '
                      '$status $body_bytes_sent "$http_referer" '
                      '"$http_user_agent" "$http_x_forwarded_for"';

    access_log  /var/log/nginx/access.log  main;

    server {
        listen       80;
        server_name  localhost;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://localhost:880;
            proxy_set_header   Host             $host;
            proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP        $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For  $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        }
    }
}
```

This configuration defines a server block that listens on port 80, which is the default HTTP port. It sets up a log format (`main`) that records various details about each request, including the IP address of the client, the request method and path, the status code of the response, and other information. The `access_log` directive specifies where these logs should be written.

The `server` block then defines a single server that responds to requests on the default server name (`localhost`). The `location /` block is where the proxying happens. It proxies requests to `http://localhost:880`, which means any request to the root URL (`/`) on port 80 will be forwarded to the server listening on port 880 on the same machine.

